Who is the most important person in the kingdom?
At that time the disciples came to Jesus and asked him, "Who is the most important in the kingdom of heaven?"
Jesus called a little child and had him stand among them.
Jesus said, "What I have to tell you is true. You must change and become like little children. If you don't, you will not enter the kingdom of heaven."
Everyone who becomes humble like this child is the greatest in the kingdom of heaven.
"Whoever welcomes such a little child in my name, welcomes me."
But what if one of these little ones who believe in me causes them to sin? If he causes them to sin, it would be better for him to have a large millstone hung around his neck and be drowned at the bottom of the sea.
How dreadful to the world will be those things that bring people into sin! Such things must come; but alas for those by whom they come!
Your hands or your feet are a temptation, so cut them off. Indeed, the fire in Hell remains forever.
If anyone wants to gouge out his eye to see, let him gouge it out and throw it away. It is better for him to enter the kingdom of God with one eye, than to be thrown into hell with two eyes. The story of the lost sheep.
Look, don't look at any of these little ones condescendingly, behold, I say to you, they have angels in heaven who rise at any time to see my Father who is in heaven.
<.>
What is your opinion? Imagine a man who owns 100 sheep, and one of them goes astray. Should he not leave the ninety-nine sheep on the mountain? Should he not go and search for the one that has gone astray?
What I am going to tell you is true. If he finds that lost sheep, he will be happier with it than he is with those 99 that have not strayed.
And it is so with your father in heaven. He does not want any of these little ones to perish. When someone wrongs you
If your brother wrongs you, go to him. Tell him what he did wrong. Make that between the two of you. If he listens to you, he has returned to you.
But what if he will not listen to you? Take one or two more persons with you. The Book says: "Every fact must be confirmed by the words of two or three witnesses" (Deut. 19:15).
But what if he also refuses to listen to witnesses? Then tell the church. And what if he refuses to listen even to the church? Then do not treat him as a brother. Treat him as you would treat someone who does not believe or a tax collector.
What I will tell you is true. What you bind on earth will be bound in heaven. What you loose on earth will be loosed in heaven.
And again, here is what I tell you. If two of you agree on earth about something they ask me, I will do it for them from myself.
"Wherever two or three are gathered in my name, I am there among them." The servant who did not show mercy.
Peter came to Jesus and asked, "Lord, how many times must I forgive my brother when he sins against me? Up to seven times?"
Jesus said, "I tell you, not seven times, but seventy-seven times.
The kingdom of heaven is like a king who wanted to settle accounts with his servants.
And the king began to do that, and a man was presented to him who owed him millions of dollars.
The man could not pay. So his master gave the order. The man, his wife, his children, and everything he possessed, had to be sold to pay his debt.
The servant knelt before him. "Give me time," he pleaded. "I will pay everything."
His lord felt pity for him. He forgave him the debt he owed and set him free.
But the slave went out and found one of the other slaves, to whom he owed a few dollars. So he seized him and began to strangle him. 'Pay me what you owe me!' he said.
The other server fell to his knees. 'Give me time,' he pleaded with him. 'I will return the favor.'
But the first servant refused. Instead, he went and had the man thrown into prison. And there he would stay until he could pay back what he owed.
The other servants saw what happened. This disturbed them greatly. They went and told their master everything that had happened.
Then the Lord called His first servant and said, "You wicked servant! I forgave you your debt that you could not pay."
Haven't you been merciful to the other servant just as you have been merciful to me?
His master unleashed his wrath upon him, handing him over to the jailers. He would be punished until he repaid all his debts.
Thus your Father who is in heaven treats everyone, unless you forgive your brothers from your hearts.
Jesus teaches about divorce.
When Jesus had finished saying these things, he left Galilee and went into the region of Judea beyond the Jordan River.
Large crowds followed him. He healed them there.
Some Pharisees came to test him. They asked, "Is it lawful for a man to divorce his wife for just any reason?"
Jesus said, "Have they not read in the Scriptures: 'In the beginning, God created the heavens and the earth, and God created man in his own image, male and female' (Genesis 1:27)?"
He said, "Therefore a man shall leave his father and mother and be joined to his wife, and they shall become one flesh" (Genesis 2:24).
What was once separate has become one. Therefore, a man should not tear asunder what God has joined together.
They asked, "Why then did Moses command the man to write a divorce document for his wife and send her away?"
Jesus answered, "Moses allowed you to divorce because of the hardness of your hearts. But it was not like that in the beginning."
This is what I tell you. Anyone who divorces his wife and marries another commits adultery. A man should not divorce his wife unless she is not faithful to him.
The disciples said to him, "If such is the case between husband and wife, it is better not to marry."
Jesus answered, "Not everyone can accept the idea of remaining single. Only those whom someone has helped to live without marrying can accept it.
Some men cannot have children because they were born that way. Some had others make them that way. And others made themselves that way to serve the Kingdom of Heaven. And whoever can accept living that way, must do so." The little children come to Jesus.
Some people brought two children to Jesus. They wanted him to lay his hands on the children and pray for them. But the disciples told the people to stop.
Jesus said, "Let the little children come to me, and do not hinder them, for the kingdom of God belongs to such as these."
Jesus laid his hands on them. Then he left from there. Jesus and the Rich Young Man
A man came to Jesus. And he asked, "Teacher, what good thing must I do to get eternal life?"
"Why do you ask me about what is good?" Jesus answered. "There is only one who is good. If you want to enter into the kingdom, obey the commandments."
"Which?" the man asked. Jesus said, "Do not murder. Do not commit adultery. Do not steal. Do not give false testimony against your neighbor."
"Honor your father and your mother." (Exodus 20:12-16; Deuteronomy 5:16-20) and "Love your neighbor as yourself." (Leviticus 19:18)
"I have obeyed all those commands," said the young man. "What should I do next?"
Jesus answered, "If you want to be perfect, go, sell your possessions and give the money to the poor, and you will have treasure in heaven. Then come, follow me."
When the young man heard that, he left sadly. He was very rich.
Then Jesus said to his disciples: "What I tell you now is true. It is hard for those who have wealth to enter the Kingdom of Heaven."
And I tell you again, it is easier for a camel to go through the eye of a needle. It is easier for a rich person to enter the kingdom of God.
When the disciples heard this, they confirmed it. So they said, "Who then can be saved?"
Jesus looked at them and said, "What is impossible for man is possible for God."
Peter answered him, "We have left everything to follow you! What will be our reward?"
"I am not yet worthy to speak to you. But the time is coming when the world will be renewed, and then the Son of Man will sit on his glorious throne. And those who have followed me will also sit on twelve thrones, judging the twelve tribes of Israel."
Everyone who has left a house or family or field because of me will receive 100 times more. And they will also receive eternal life.
Many who are first will be last, and many who are last will be first.
The Story of the Workers in the Vineyard
The kingdom of heaven is like a man who owned a vineyard. He went out early in the morning to hire workers for his vineyard.
He agreed to pay them the usual wage for a day's work. Then he sent them into his vineyard.
He went out again around nine in the morning. He saw others standing in the city market doing nothing.
He said to them, "Go also and work in my vineyard, and I will give you a just wage."
They went out. "He went out again around noon and at three and did the same thing.
He went out around five o'clock and found others standing around it. He asked them, "Why have you been standing here all day doing nothing?"
They answered, "Because no one has commissioned us." And he said to them, "You also go to my field and work in it."
When evening came, the owner of the vineyard spoke to the supervisor of the workers. He said, "Call the workers and give them their pay. Start with the others who were hired. Then go to the first ones."
The workers who were hired around five o'clock arrived. Each of them received their usual day's wage.
Therefore, when those who had been hired first came, they expected to receive more. But each of them also received the pay for a normal day.
When they received it, they began to complain about the landlord.
'These people who were recently hired only worked for one hour,' they said. 'You paid them the same amount that you paid us. We did most of the work. We were under the hot sun all day.'
One of them the master answered: My friend, I am being honest with you. Did you not agree to work for the usual day's wage?
Take your money and go. I want to give whoever I hire last the same salary I gave you.
Do I not have the right to do as I please with my money? Do you feel cheated because I have given generously to others?
"Therefore the last will be first, and the first will be last." Jesus, once again, speaks of his impending death.
Jesus was going up to Jerusalem. And on the way, he took the twelve disciples aside to talk to them.
"We are going up to Jerusalem," he said. "The Son of Man will be delivered over to the chief priests and the scribes. They will condemn him to death."
Then they hand him over to the people who are not Jewish, who mock him and flog him, and then they crucify him on a cross. And on the third day, he rises from the dead! A mother begging Jesus
The mother of the sons of Zebedee came to Jesus with her sons. She knelt down and asked him for a request.
"What do you want?" Jesus asked. "Promise me," she said, "that one of my sons will sit at your right in your kingdom. And promise me that the other will sit at your left."
"You do not know what you are asking," Jesus said to them. "Can you drink the cup of suffering that I am to drink?" "Yes, we can," they answered.
Jesus said to them, "Assuredly, you will drink from my cup; and how I wish that none of you would drink it! But I have no choice but to accept the cup the Father has given me. And how I wish that none of you would have to drink from it! For my part, my Father is glorified by this. And he will glorify the Son, by whose name I have been given everything, from the Father. So Jesus said, 'The Father is glorified by this: that you bear much fruit and show yourselves to be my disciples. I have glorified you on earth. I have finished the work you gave me to do. And now, Father, glorify me in your presence with the glory I had with you before the world was made. You gave me glory on earth, and now give me my glory in heaven with you. So my Father glorified me, because I served the purpose he gave me to do. And now, my children, remain in me, just as I remain in you. No branch can bear fruit by itself; it must remain in the vine. And as the branch cannot bear fruit by itself, unless it remains in the vine, so you cannot bear fruit unless you remain in me. Just as the branch is fully part of the vine, so also you can fully experience the reality of my presence within you. You can fully experience the reality of my presence within you, just as the branch fully experiences the reality of its connection to the vine. As the Father loves me, so I also love you. Remain in my love. If you keep my commands, you will remain in my love, just as I have kept my Father's commands and remain in his love. I have revealed myself to you, and I continue to reveal myself to you, so that you may have lasting joy in the presence of God. This is my commandment: that you love one another, just as I have loved you. You are to love one another, just as I have loved you. Greater love has no one than this: that someone lay down his life for his friends. You are my friends if you do what I command. No longer do I call you servants, for a servant does not know what his master is doing; but I have called you friends, for all that I have heard from the Father I have made known to you. You did not choose me, but I chose you and appointed you to go and bear fruit, and your fruit will last; so that whatever you ask the Father in my name, he may give it to you. This is I command you: that you love one another. If the world hates you, realize that it hated me before it hated you. If you belonged to the world, the world would love you; but because you do not belong to the world, but I have chosen you out of the world - therefore the world hates you. Remember what I told you: 'A servant is not greater than his master.' If they persecuted me, they will persecute you also. In this world you will have trouble; but take heart, I have overcome the world.
The other ten pupils heard that. They were angry with the two brothers.
Jesus called his disciples and said, "You know that the rulers of the Gentiles lord it over them, and their great ones exercise authority over them. But it shall not be so among you; but whoever desires to become great among you, let him be your servant, and whoever desires to be first among you, let him be your slave, even as the Son of Man was not to be served but to serve, and to give His life as a ransom for many."
Do not be like this. Rather, whoever among you wants to be important must be your servant.
And whoever wants to be first, let him be your slave.
Be like the Son of Man. He did not come to be served, but to serve others. He came to give his life as a ransom for many." Two blind men receive their sight.
Jesus and his disciples were leaving Jericho. A large crowd followed them.
There were two blind men sitting by the road. When they heard that Jesus was passing by, they cried out, saying, "Lord, son of David, have mercy on us!"
The crowd ordered them to stop. They told them to be silent. But the two men shouted even louder, "Lord! Son of David! Have mercy on us!"
Jesus stopped and called to them, "What do you want me to do for you?" he asked.
"Our Lord," they answered. "We want to be able to see."
Jesus felt deep compassion for them. He touched their eyes, and they saw immediately. They followed him.
Jesus enters Jerusalem.
When they all approached Jerusalem, they came to Bethphage, which was on the Mount of Olives. Jesus sent two disciples ahead.
He said to them, "Go to the village before you. As soon as you enter it, you will find a donkey with its foal tied to it. Untie them and bring them both to me."
If anyone tells you something, say the Lord needs them. The owner will send them immediately.
This happened so that what came out of the mouth of the Prophet would be fulfilled. It says,
"Tell this to the daughter of Zion: 'Behold, your king is coming to you, righteous and victorious, lowly and riding on a donkey, and on a colt, the foal of a donkey.'"" (Zechariah 9:9)
The disciples went and did what Jesus had commanded them.
They brought a donkey and a female foal. They placed their cloaks on them. Then Jesus sat on the two cloaks.
A very large crowd of people spread out on the road and opened their coats. Others cut branches from the trees and spread them on the road.
Some people went ahead of him, and some followed. And they all cried out, saying, "Blessed is he who comes in the name of the Lord!" (Psalm 118:26) "Blessed in the highest heavens!"
When Jesus entered Jerusalem, he shook the whole city. And the people asked, "Who is this?"
And the crowd answered, "This is Jesus of Nazareth, the prophet." Jesus Cleanses the Temple
Jesus entered the temple court. And he began to drive out those who were buying and selling there. And he overturned the tables of the money changers, and the chairs of those who sold pigeons.
He said to them: "It is written: 'The Lord said: My house shall be called a house of prayer.' (Isaiah 56:7) But you have made it 'a den of thieves.' (Matthew 21:13)'"
The blind and those who had deformities came to Jesus at the temple. And he healed them there.
The chief priests and the teachers of the law saw the wonderful miracles he did. They also saw the children in the temple area shouting, "Praise God! Bless the son of David!" But the chief priests and teachers of the law were angry when they saw all this.
"Do you hear what these children are saying?" they asked Him. "Yes," Jesus answered. "Have you not read that in the book? It says: 'Because He has set His love firmly on Me, therefore He will deliver Me from the power of Sheol, and will deliver Me from death, and my life will see the light.'" (Psalm 30:1-3)
Then Jesus went out from the crowd and went into the house of Simon the leper. He spent the night there. The disciples of John asked him, "Why do we and the Pharisees fast, but your disciples do not fast?" Jesus said, "The attendants of the bridegroom cannot mourn as long as the bridegroom is with them, but they will later fast. No one sews a patch of unshrunk cloth onto an old garment; otherwise, the new patch will pull away from the old garment, and the tear will become even worse. The disciples of John also used to fast. People came to Jesus and saw the blind man walking with them, and they asked, "What happened to this man?" Some people said, "He is John the Baptist; he has risen from the dead, and that is why these miraculous signs are happening through him." Others said, "He is Elijah." And others said, "He is a prophet, like one of the prophets of old." But when Herod heard this, he was deeply disturbed; and he suspected that John had somehow been raised from the dead. Herod had arrested John and bound him and put him in prison because of Herodias, the wife of Herod's brother Philip. John had told Herod, "It is not lawful for you to have your brother's wife." Herodias harbored a grudge against John and wanted to kill him, but she could not do so. When Herod celebrated his birthday with a great feast for his nobles and military commanders and for the leading men of Galilee, the daughter of Herodias came forward and danced, and she pleased Herod and his guests. The king said to the girl, "Whatever you ask of me, I will give you." He swore to her, "As surely as God lives, what you ask for I will give you." She went out and said to her mother, "What should I ask for?" Her mother replied, "The head of John the Baptist." The girl went back in quickly to the king and stood before him. "I will pay you whatever you ask," the king said to her. "Half of my kingdom," she answered, "or, if you like, the head of John the Baptist on a platter." The king was distressed, but because of his oaths and his guests, he was unable to refuse her. Immediately the king sent a soldier of the guard with orders to bring John's head. The soldier went out, and while he was still on his way, John said to the two disciples who were with him, "Look, the man who is coming to meet us is the one who is going to betray me. But it would be better for that man if he had never been born." As John said this, the disciple whom Jesus loved said to Peter, "Look, the Lord is going to show himself to us, just as he said." Peter said to John, "Teacher, tell this man to stop what he is doing. It is not right for him to do this." John replied, "Don't hinder him, for no one who does a miracle in my name can be against me. Whoever is not with me is against me, and whoever does not gather with me scatters. I tell you the truth, anyone who accepts my message will never see death." Then Jesus said, "The hour has come, and now is, when the Son of Man will be glorified. I tell you the truth, unless a kernel of wheat falls to the ground and dies, it remains just a single seed. But if it dies, it produces many seeds, a plentiful harvest. Whoever loves his life will lose it, and whoever hates his life in this world will keep it for eternal life. What
Early in the morning, Jesus was going to Jerusalem. He was hungry.
He saw a fig tree by the road. He went up to it, but found nothing on it but leaves. Then he said to it, "May you never bear fruit!" And the fig tree withered at once.
When the disciples saw this, they were perplexed. "How has the olive tree withered so quickly?" they asked.
Jesus said, "I tell you, if you give to the needy, you will be rewarded. And if you pray, your prayers will be heard. And if you ask the mountain to cast itself into the sea, and if you have faith, it will be done for you."
"If you believe, you will receive whatever you ask for in prayer." Doubting Jesus' authority
Jesus entered the temple courts. As he taught, the chief priests and the teachers of the law came up to him. "By what authority are you doing these things?" they asked. "And who gave you this authority?"
Jesus answered and said, "I also will ask you a question. If you answer me, I also will tell you by what authority I do these things.
From where did John's baptism come? Was it from heaven, or from men?" They disputed with one another, saying, "If we say, 'From heaven,' he will say, 'Why did you not believe him?'
But what if we said, "Among men"? We are afraid of people. Everyone thought that John was a prophet.
They said to him, "We do not know." Jesus said to them, "Nor will I tell you by what authority I do these things." The Parable of the Two Sons
What do you think about this? There was a man who had two sons. He went to the first one and said, "My son, go and work today in the vineyard."
"I will not," the son answered. But afterwards he changed his mind and went.
Then the father went to the other son. He told him the same thing. The son replied, "I will, Father." But he did not go.
Which of the two sons did the will of his father?" They said, "The first." Jesus said to them, "Truly, I say to you, tax collectors and prostitutes enter the kingdom of God ahead of you."
John came to show you the right way to live. But you did not believe him. The tax collectors and prostitutes, however, did believe him. You saw that. But even then you did not stop your sins and believe him. The story of the tenants.
Listen to another story. A man owned some land, so he planted vineyards. He put a wall around them. And he dug a reservoir in them for pressing wine. He also built a watchtower. And he rented the vineyards to some farmers. Then he traveled on a journey.
When the harvest time approached, he sent his servants to the tenants. And he commanded the servants to gather his share of the fruits.
But the tenants seized his servants. They beat one of them. They killed another. They threw a stone at a third, intending to kill him.
Then the man sent other servants to the tenants. He sent more than he had sent at first. The tenants treated them in the same way.
And finally, He sent His Son to them and said, "They will respect my Son."
But the tenants saw the son coming. They said to each other, "This is the one who will take over all the owner's possessions someday. Let's kill him. Then everything will be ours."
And they took him and cast him out of the vineyard. Then they killed him.
When did the owner of the vineyard come? What is he doing with these tenants?
"They will tear down those evil people's house," they answered. "Then the field will be rented to others. They will give him his share of the harvest at the time of the harvest."
Jesus said to them, "Has it not been written in the Law, 'The stone which the builders rejected has become the head of the corner. This is from the Lord, and it is wonderful in our eyes'? (Psalm 118:22, 23)"
Here is what I say to you. The kingdom of God will be taken from you, and it will be given to a people that produces its fruits.
Everyone who falls on that rock will be shattered into pieces. But the rock will crush anyone it falls on.
The chief priests and the Pharisees heard reports about Jesus. They knew he was speaking about them.
So they looked for a way to arrest him. But they feared the crowd. The people believed that Jesus was a prophet.
The Wedding Dinner Story
Jesus told them more stories. He said:
This is the parable of the Kingdom of Heaven. A king prepared a wedding feast for his son.
Send a service to whoever was invited to dinner. Tell them to come. But they refused.
Then he sent more servants than the first. He said, 'Tell those who are invited, "I have prepared a dinner for you. I have already killed the sheep and the cattle." Everything is ready.' So come to the wedding dinner.'
But the people did not care. One of them went to his field. And another went to his business.
The rest seized his servants. They treated him badly and then killed him.
The king was furiously angry. He sent his army to destroy them. They killed those murderers and burned their city.
Then the king said to his servants, "The reading for the wedding has been prepared. But those whom I invited were not worthy of attending."
Go to the street corners. Invite to dinner anyone you find.
The servants went out into the streets and gathered everyone they found, good and bad people. Soon the wedding hall was filled with guests.
The king entered to see the guests. He noticed a man there who was not wearing wedding clothes.
"My friend, how did you get in here without your wedding clothes?" The man found nothing to say.
Then he gestured to his servants, and they restrained him and threw him into the cloud. They went out with him to a distant place. And there the people weep and bite their fingers.
Many are called, but few are chosen. Is it right to pay taxes to Caesar?
The Pharisees went out and immediately took counsel with the Herodians against Jesus, to trap him in his words.
They sent their followers to him. They sent the Herodians with them. "Teacher," they said, "we know that you are a good and honest man. You truly seek to learn the ways of God. You do not let others tell you what to do or say. It does not matter how important they are."
So tell us, what do you think? Is it right to pay taxes to Caesar or not?"
But Jesus perceived their wicked thoughts. He said to them, "You hypocrites! What schemes are you trying to use to entrap me?"
"Show me the coin that people use to pay taxes." And they brought him a silver coin.
He asked them, "Whose picture is this? And whose words are these?"
"For Caesar," they said. Then he said to them, "Render to Caesar the things that are Caesar's, and to God the things that are God's."
When they heard this, it was distressing to them. So they departed, and they were negotiating. A marriage when the dead rise.
On that day, the Samaritans came to Jesus with a question. They do not believe that people rise from the dead.
"O teacher," they said, "this is what Moses commanded us. If a man dies without leaving children, his brother shall marry his widow. Thus, he shall produce children for his brother.
There were seven brothers among us. The first got married and died. Since he had no children, he left his wife for his brother.
The same thing happened to the second brother, and the third brother. It even happened to the seventh brother.
Finally, the woman died.
Well, when the dead rise, who will be his wife? All seven were married to her.
Jesus answered, "You are mistaken, because you do not know the Scriptures or the power of God."
When the dead rise, they will not marry, nor will their fathers give them away in marriage; for they will be like the angels in heaven.
What about the dead being raised? Have you not read what God has revealed to you?
He said, "I am God, the One who appeared in a burning bush to Moses; the One who appeared to Abraham in a vision; the One who appeared to Isaac in a vision; and the One who appeared to Jacob in a vision." (Exodus 3:6) "Now the God of the living, not the God of the dead, you are in error."
When the crowd heard this, they were bewildered at what he was teaching. Which commandment is the most important?
The Pharisees heard that the Samaritans could not answer Jesus. So the Pharisees gathered together.
One of them was an expert in the law. He tested Jesus with a question.
«Teacher,» he asked, «which commandment in the law is the most important?»
Jesus answered, "Blessed are those who love the Lord and love His mercy" (Deuteronomy 6:5).
This is the first and most important commandment.
And a similar one: "You shall love your neighbor as yourself" (Leviticus 19:18).
All that was written in the Torah and the Gospel about these two commandments. Who is the son of Christ?
The Pharisees met together. Jesus asked them,
What do you think about the Messiah? Who are his builders?" They said, "The son of David."
He said to them, "Then why does David call him 'Lord'?" The Holy Spirit spoke through David himself. David said,
'The Lord said to my Lord, "Sit at my right hand until I put your enemies under your feet"' (Psalm 110:1)
If David calls him "sir", how can he be his son?
No one could answer him with a single word. And from that day on, no one dared to ask him any questions.
Jesus condemns the Pharisees and teachers of the law.
Jesus spoke to the crowds and to his disciples.
"The scribes and Pharisees sit in Moses' seat," He said.
Therefore, you must obey them. Do everything they command you to do. But do not do what they do. They do not practice what they preach.
They tie burdens on the shoulders of others. But they themselves do not wish to lift a single finger to move them.
All they do is done to be seen by others. And on their foreheads and arms they put small boxes containing verses from the Bible. And they make the boxes very wide. And they make the strings hanging from their coats very long.
They love to sit in the place of honor at banquets. They also love to be in the most important seats in temples.
They like to be welcomed into the markets. They like to be called "Rabbi".
But none of you should be called 'Lord.' You have only one Lord, and you are all brothers."
Do not say 'father' to anyone on earth. For you have one Father, and He is in heaven.
None of you should be called teacher. You have one teacher, the Christ.
Your most important people are your servants.
He who exalts himself will be humbled, and he who humbles himself will be exalted.
"How bad it will be for you, teachers of the law and you, Pharisees! You are hypocrites! You lock up the kingdom of heaven in the face of people. And you yourselves do not enter, nor do you allow those who try to enter."
<.>
"How awful it is for you, teachers of the law and you, Pharisees! You are hypocrites! You go all over the world to convert one person to your religion. Then you make him twice as much a child of hell as you are.
"How terrible that was for you blind guides! You say, 'If anyone has sworn by the temple, they are free of obligation. But anyone who swears by the gold in the temple must make good on their vow.'"
You are blind and stupid! Which is more important? The gold? Or is it the temple that makes the gold sacred?
You also say, "He who swears by the altar, nothing. But whoever swears by the gift that is on it, let him be bound by the oath."
Blind guides! Which is more important? The gift? Or the altar to which the gift makes its way, becoming sacred?
For whoever swears by the altar, swears by it and by everything on it.
And everyone who swears by the name of the temple, swears by his name and by the one who dwells in it.
And everyone who swears by heaven swears by the name of God's throne and by the one who sits on it.
"How wretched were you teachers of the law and Pharisees! You impostors! You give God a tenth of your garden herbs, such as mint, dill, and cumin. But you have neglected the more important matters of the law, such as justice, mercy, and faithfulness. You should have practiced the latter without neglecting to do the former."
You blind guides! You filter out the smallest gnat from your drink. But you swallow a whole camel!
Woe to you, teachers of the law and you, Pharisees, hypocrites! You are like tombs, which look beautiful on the outside but inside are full of dead bones and everything unclean. Even so, on the outside you appear righteous to people, but inside you are full of hypocrisy and lawlessness.
O blind Pharisee! Cleanse first what is within the cup and the plate, then what is outside will be clean also.
How awful it was for you, teachers of the law and Pharisees! You are hypocrites! You are like whitewashed tombs. They look beautiful on the outside. But inside they are full of the bones of the dead. So also, on the outside they appear as righteous people. But on the inside, they are full of hypocrisy and lawlessness.
This is the case with you. From the outside, it looks like you are doing the right thing. But on the inside, you are full of wrong. You pretend to be what you are not.
How awful it is for you, teachers of the law and you Pharisees! You hypocrites! You build tombs for the prophets. You decorate the tombs of the righteous.
And you say: If only we had lived in the times of those who lived before us, we would not have followed their path, nor would we have turned to them, what they were doing.
You have testified against yourselves, and you are now testifying that you are the sons of those who killed the prophets.
Complete the sins that those before you began!
O nest of vipers! How will you escape the punishment of hell?
Therefore, I send you prophets and wise men and teachers. You will kill some of them, and you will crucify others on a cross. And you will flog some in your churches. And you will chase them from town to town.
Therefore you will pay for all the blood of the holy ones that has been poured out on the earth, from the blood of righteous Abel to the blood of Zechariah, the son of Barachiah, whom you murdered between the temple and the altar.
What I will tell you is true. All this will happen to those who are living now.
Jerusalem! Jerusalem! You who kill the prophets and stone those sent to you. I have often wanted to gather your people, as a hen gathers her brood under her wings. But you would not let me!
Look, you left your house empty.
I tell you, you will not see me until you say, "Blessed is he who comes in the name of the Lord." (Psalm 118:26)
