That was in the early morning. All the chief priests and the elders of the people decided to put Jesus to death.
They bound him and dragged him away. Then they handed him over to Pilate, the governor.
Judas Iscariot, who was betraying him, saw that Jesus was condemned to die. This filled him with remorse and he was overcome with deep sorrow for what he had done. He took the thirty silver coins back to the chief priests and elders.
"I have made a mistake," he said. "I have handed over an innocent man." "What does it matter to us," they replied. "This is your problem."
Then Judas threw the money into the temple and departed. Then he went and hanged himself from a rope.
The priests of the temple picked up the coins. "We have committed a crime by putting this money in the temple chest," they said. "It is blood money. It has paid for a man's death."
Therefore, they decided to use the money to buy a columbarium. People coming from other countries will be buried there.
Therefore, to this day, it is called the Field of Blood.
Then the words of Jeremiah the prophet appeared. He said, "They have taken the two and twenty pieces of silver, the price of the one whose price our God set." And I said, "What do these men, the ones who are burying the one whose name the Lord called to account, mean by these things?" And one of the elders who stood before the king said to me, "I will tell you what these things mean. The two and twenty pieces of silver, the price of the one whose price our God set, was taken by the potter, just as the Lord had told me." And I, even I, Daniel, was grieved within my spirit, and the visions of my head troubled me. And I went to one of those who stood by, and drew near to him and asked him, "Who is the great ruler of these ruins?" And he said, "The great ruler of the ruins is the god of forces, even the god whom the people of Israel worshipped. And they carried their idols with them, and even their idols and precious vessels of silver and gold. They came to Babylon and entered into the house of their idols, and they bowed down and burned incense and offered their sacrifices. And when the God of heaven saw what they were doing, he sent his angel and destroyed those people and destroyed that great city. And it became as dry as the wilderness, and all its splendor was completely vanished. And the nations all over the world all became exceedingly afraid and trembled greatly, and no one dared to say anything against them or to criticize them for a long time thereafter.
They used the silver pieces to buy the potter's field, as the Lord instructed me." (Zechariah 11:12,13; Jeremiah 19:1-13; 32:6-9) Jesus is Brought Before Pilate
Jesus was standing before the governor. The governor asked him, "Are you the king of the Jews?" Jesus answered, "Yes, it is as you say."
But when the high priest and the elders accused him, he gave no answer.
Then Pilate said to him, "Do you not hear the testimony they are bearing against you?"
But Jesus answered not a word to any of the charges. The governor was exceedingly astonished.
It was the custom of the governor at Passover to release a single prisoner. The people would choose whom they wanted.
At that time, they had a famous prisoner named Bapparra.
When their governor had assembled them, he said to them, "Whom do you want me to release for you? Barabbas? Or Jesus who is called the Messiah?"
Pilatess realized that the leaders were envious. He realized that this was why Jesus was handed over to him.
While Pilatus was sitting on the judge's chair, his wife sent him a message. The message read: "Don't involve him in anything. He is not guilty. I suffered a lot in my dream today because of this man."
But the chief priests and the elders persuaded the crowd to ask for Barabbas and to have Jesus executed.
"Which of the two do I release unto you?" asked the governor. "Barabbas," they answered.
"What then shall I do with Jesus called Christ?" Pilate asked. They all answered, "Crucify him!"
"Why? What has he done?" Pilate asked. But they shouted all the louder, "Crucify him!"
Pilate saw that he was getting nowhere. Indeed, the crowd was becoming angry. So he washed his hands in full view of them all. "I am innocent of this man's death," he declared. "It is your responsibility!"
Everyone answered: "We and our children will bear the responsibility for his death!"
Pilate released Baruch. But he flogged Jesus. Then he handed him over to be crucified on the cross. The guards mocked Jesus.
The governor's soldiers took Jesus to the palace, which was called the Praetorium. And all the other soldiers gathered around him.
They stripped him and put a purple robe on him.
Then they gathered thorns and made a crown of them. They put it on his head. They put a reed in his right hand. Then they knelt down before him and mocked him. "We honor you, O King of the Jews!" they said.
They cursed him. Someone hit him repeatedly on the head with a stick, over and over again.
After mocking him, they took off his robe. They put his clothes on him. Then they led him to be crucified on the cross. The crucifixion of Jesus.
And when they went out of the city, they found a man of Cyrene named Simon, they compelled him to bear the cross of Jesus and walk behind him.
They reached a place called Golgotha. The word Golgotha means "the place of the skull."
There they mixed the wine with types of bitter spices and gave it to Jesus to drink. And after he tasted it, he refused to drink it.
When they crucified him, they divided his garments among them by casting lots.
They sat down, guarding him there.
They placed over his head a sign that read: "This is Jesus, the King of the Jews."
He was trapped with two robbers, one of them on his right and the other on his left.
Those who passed by there cursed Jesus and ridiculed him. They shook their heads
And he said, "Are you going to destroy the sanctuary and rebuild it in three days? Save yourself! Come down from the cross, if you are the Son of God!"
Likewise, the chief priests, the teachers of the law and the elders mocked him.
"They saved the others," they said. "But he cannot save himself! He is the king of Israel! Let him come down from the cross now! Then we will believe in him."
He trusts in God. Let God save him now if He wills it. It is he who said: "I am the Son of God."
Likewise, the thieves who were crucified with Jesus mocked Jesus. Jesus' death
From noon until three o'clock, darkness covered the entire earth.
About the third hour, Jesus cried out with a loud voice, "Eloi, Eloi, lama sabachthani?" which means, "My God, my God, why have you forsaken me?" (Psalm 22:1)
Some of those who stood there heard Jesus cry out. They said, "He is calling for Elijah."
Immediately, one of them took a reed, dipped it in vinegar, fixed it to a spear, and held it up to Jesus for him to drink.
The rest said, "Leave him alone. Let's see if Elijah will come to save him."
After Jesus cried out again in a loud voice, he died.
At that moment, the curtain of the temple was torn from top to bottom. The earth shook. The rocks split.
The tombs opened. The bodies of many saints who had died were brought back to life.
They came out of the tombs. After the Lord Jesus was destined to raise from the dead, He ascended to the holy city. And there He appeared to many.
The centurion and those who were with him guarding Jesus, saw the earthquake and what happened, and they were filled with great fear and said, "Truly, he was the Son of God!"
It wasn't too far, there were many women watching. They had followed Jesus from Galilee to care for his needs.
Mary Magdalene was among them. Mary, the mother of James and Joseph, was there also. And the mother of the sons of Zebedee. Jesus was buried.
When evening came, a wealthy man from the city of Arimathea came. His name was Joseph. He had become a follower of Jesus.
He went to Pilate and asked for Jesus' body. Pilate ordered that he be given it.
Joseph took the body and wrapped it in a clean linen cloth.
He placed it in his new tomb, which he had carved out of the rock. He pushed a large stone in front of the entrance to the tomb. Then he went away.
Mary Magdalene and the other woman sat opposite the tomb. The guards were at the tomb.
And the next day was after the day of the preparation. So the chief priests and the Pharisees came to Pilate.
"Sir," they said, "we recall something the liar said while he was alive. He claimed, 'After three days I will rise again.'"
Give us an order that they make the tomb secure until the third day; otherwise, his disciples may come and steal the body; then they will go and tell the people that Jesus has been raised from the dead; and this last lie will be worse than the first.
"Take some guards with you," Pilate answered. "Go. Make the tomb as secure as possible."
So they went and made the grave secure. They placed a seal on the stone and put guards to watch.
Jesus ascends to heaven.
Saturday had come to an end. Dawn had broken on the first day of the week. Mary Magdalene and the other woman went to check on the tomb.
There was a strong earthquake. An angel of the Lord descended from heaven. The angel went to the tomb. The stone returned and sat upon it.
His body was glowing like lightning. His clothes were white as snow.
The guards were shaking with fear of him until they became like the dead.
The angel said to the woman, "Do not be afraid, I know that you are looking for Jesus who was crucified."
He is not here! He has gone up, just as he said he would! Come and see the place where he was lying.
Go quickly! Tell his disciples, "He has been raised from the dead. He is going before you to Galilee. There you will see him." This is what I told you.
The women rushed out from the tomb, greatly frightened, yet filled with joy. They ran to tell the disciples.
Jesus came and said to them: "Peace be with you." They approached him and took hold of his feet and worshipped him.
Then Jesus said to them, "Do not be afraid. Go and tell my brothers to go to Galilee, and there they will see me." The guards went back to the chief priests and the Pharisees.
While the women were on their way, some of the guards entered the city. They reported to the chief priests everything that had happened.
When the high priests met with the elders, they made a plan. They gave the soldiers a large sum of money.
They said to the soldiers: "We want you to say: 'His disciples came at night and stole his body while we were sleeping.'"
If the Governor hears this report, we will push it. This will keep you out of trouble.
So the soldiers took the money and did as they were instructed. This story has spread all over among the Jews to this day. Jesus' last instructions to his disciples
Then the eleventh Christian disciple went to Galilee. So they went to the mountain as Jesus had instructed them.
When they saw him, they bowed down to him, but some of them had doubts.
Then Jesus came to them and said, "All authority in heaven and on earth has been given to me."
Go then and baptize all nations in the name of the Father, and of the Son, and of the Holy Spirit.
Teach them to obey you whenever I command you. And be sure that I will be with you always until the end.
John the Baptist paves the way.
This is the beginning of the good news about Jesus Christ, the Son of God.
Isaiah the prophet wrote long ago: "I send My messenger to prepare the way before you" (Malachi 3:1).
A voice cries out in the desert: "Prepare the way of the Lord, make straight paths in the wilderness." (Isaiah 40:3)
And so John came. He baptized people in the desert. He also preached that people should repent and turn away from their sins. Then God would forgive them.
All the people of the countryside of Judea went out to him, and all the people of Jerusalem went out to him; and they confessed their sins, and he immersed them in the river Jordan.
John wore clothes made of camel hair. He had a leather belt wrapped around his waist. He ate locusts and wild honey.
This is what John used to preach. "After me, one will come who is mightier than I. I am not good enough to stoop down and untie his sandals."
I baptize you with water, but he will baptize you with the Holy Spirit." The baptism and testing of Jesus.
At that time Jesus came from Nazareth in Galilee. John baptized him in the Jordan River.
Jesus was coming up out of the water. And at that moment, he looked up toward heaven, and the heavens were opened for him. And he saw the Holy Spirit descending upon him like a dove.
A voice spoke to him from heaven. It said, "You are my son, and I love you. Take great delight in me."
Jesus then bid farewell to the Holy Spirit, and went off into the wilderness.
He stayed in the wilderness for forty days. There, he was tempted by the devil. Wild animals did not harm Jesus. Angels took care of him. Jesus chooses the first disciples.
After John was imprisoned, Jesus went into Galilee, preaching the gospel of God.
"It is time," he said. "The Kingdom of God is near. Turn away from your sins and believe in the good news!"
One day Jesus was walking along the shore of the Sea of Galilee. There he saw Simon and his brother Andrew. They were casting their net into the lake. They were fishermen.
"Come, follow me," Jesus said. "I will make you fishers of men."
So they left their nets and followed him immediately.
Then Jesus walked a little further. And immediately there was a great storm on the lake, so that the boat was covered with waves; but Jesus was asleep.
Jesus rebuked them immediately. So they left their father Zebedee in the boat with the hired men, and they followed Jesus. Jesus drives out an unclean spirit.
Jesus and his disciples went to Capernaum. And when the Sabbath day came, he entered the synagogue. And there he began to teach.
The people were astonished at his teaching; for he taught them as one having authority, and not as the scribes.
In that hour, a man in the lighthouse screamed. He was bewitched by an evil demon. He said:
"What do you want from us, Jesus of Nazareth? Have you come to destroy us? I know who you are. You are the holy one of God!"
"Be quiet!" Jesus said firmly. "Come out of him!"
The evil spirit violently shook the man. Then it came out of him, screaming.
And the people were amazed by him. And they said, "So, what you have brought with you from before, and what you are not yourselves displaying, is this what you command the heavens with, and they respond to you, and you command the earth and it throws out its judgments and what it has manufactured?"
The news about Jesus spread rapidly throughout Galilee. Jesus healed many people.
Jesus and those who were with him went out of the temple. Then they went to the house of Simon and Andrew, with James and John.
Simon's mother-in-law was lying in bed. She was suffering from a fever. They told Jesus about her.
He went to her, took her hand, and helped her stand up. The fever left her. Then she began to serve them.
That evening, after sunset, they brought to Jesus all who were sick. And they also brought all who were possessed by demons.
All the people in the city gathered at the gate.
Jesus healed many of them. They suffered from all kinds of diseases. Jesus also drove out many demons. But he did not allow the demons to speak, because they knew who he was. Jesus prayed in a quiet place.
It was very early in the morning and still dark. Jesus got up and left the house. He went to a place where he could be alone. There he prayed.
Simon and his friends went to look for Jesus.
When they found him, they cried out: "Everyone is looking for you!"
Jesus answered, "Let us go somewhere else. I want to go to the neighboring villages. I have to preach there too. That is why I came."
So he traveled throughout all of Galilee. And he was preaching in their synagogues. And he was also saving those possessed with demons. Jesus was repeating a man with skin diseases.
A man with a skin disease came to Jesus. He knelt down and begged Jesus. He said, "If you are willing to make me 'clean,' you can."
Jesus filled his heart with concern. He reached out and touched the man. He said, "I am willing to do this. Be 'clean'!"
The disease left him immediately. He recovered.
Jesus immediately sent him away. And he gave the man a strong warning.
"Don't tell this to anyone," he said. "Go and show yourself to the priest. Make the sacrifices that Moses commanded. This will be proof to the priest and the people that you are 'clean'."
But the man went out and began to talk at once. So he began to spread the news to everyone. Jesus no longer entered a city publicly. He remained outside in deserted places. But the people still came to him from everywhere.
Jesus heals a man who cannot walk.
After a few days, Jesus entered Capernaum again. And the people heard that he had come to his house.
The people gathered together in great numbers, until there was no more room. There was not even any place outside the door. And Jesus taught them the word.
Four of the newcomers were carrying a man who could not walk.
But they couldn't get him close enough to Jesus because of the crowd. So they dug a hole through the roof above Jesus. Then they lowered the man down on a mat.
Jesus saw their faith. He said to the man, "Son, your sins are forgiven."
Some law professors were sitting there. They were thinking,
Why is this man talking like this? He's saying something very wicked! No one but God forgives sins!
Immediately Jesus knew in his spirit what they were thinking in their hearts. So he said to them, "Why are you thinking these things in your hearts?"
Is it easier to say to this man, "Your sins are forgiven"? Or to say, "Arise, take up your Qur'an and walk"?
I want you to know that the Son of Man has authority on earth to forgive sins." Then Jesus spoke to the man who could not walk.
"I tell you," he said, "arise. Take your Quran and go to your home."
The man stood up and took his prayer mat. Then he walked away while everyone was watching. Everyone was amazed. They praised God and said, "We have never seen anything like this before!" Jesus Chooses Levi
Jesus went out again beside the Sea of Galilee. A great multitude came to him. He began to teach them.
And when he was on his way to the cemetery, he saw Luqwanah ibn 'Alqamah sitting at the office of the tax collector. Jesus said to him, "Follow me." So Luqwanah got up and followed him.
Then Jesus entered the house of Lazarus to eat the Passover meal. There were many tax collectors and sinners eating with him and his disciples. They were part of the large group that followed Jesus.
There were some Pharisee teachers of the law. They saw Jesus eating with "sinners" and tax collectors. So they asked his disciples, "Why does he eat with tax collectors and 'sinners'?"
Jesus heard that. He said to them: "The healthy do not need a doctor, but the sick do. I did not come to call the obedience of those who think that they are righteous in themselves, but to the obedience of those who are righteous in themselves." They ask Jesus about fasting.
The disciples of John and the Pharisees were fasting. They came to Jesus and said to him, "The disciples of John and those of the Pharisees are fasting. But your disciples are not fasting. Why is this?"
Jesus answered, How can the guests of the bridegroom fast while he is with them? They cannot fast as long as he is with them.
But comes the time when the bridegroom will be taken from them. In that day they shall go out unto the field, but they shall not find it.
People do not repair an old garment with a piece of new cloth. For if they do this, the new piece will separate from the old, making the tear in the garment even worse.
New wine should not be poured into old wineskins. If they do, the wine will burst the wineskins. And so both the wine and the wineskins will be ruined. No, everyone should put new wine into new wineskins." Jesus is Lord of the Sabbath
One Sabbath day, Jesus was walking with his disciples through the wheat fields. And the disciples began to pluck some of the wheat spikes.
The Pharisees said to Jesus, "Behold, they are doing deeds on the Sabbath. For this reason why do you teach your disciples to do what is not fitting to be done on the Sabbath?"
He answered, "Have you never read about what David did? He and his men were hungry. They needed food."
This was when Abiathar was a great priest. David entered the house of God and ate from the holy bread. Eating from it was restricted to the priests only. David gave some of it to his men.
Jesus said to them, "It is written in the prophets, 'And it shall be for all mankind. The man of God is not under the common law.' "
Therefore the Son of Man is Lord even of the Sabbath day.
Healing on Saturday
At another time Jesus entered the synagogue. There was a man there who had a withered and deformed hand.
Some Pharisees were trying to find fault with Jesus. They were watching him closely. They wanted to see if he would heal the man on the Sabbath.
Jesus spoke to the man who had a withered and shriveled hand. He said to him, "Stand up in front of everyone."
Then Jesus said to them: "What does the law say we must do in the Sabbath day? To do good or evil? To save life or to destroy it?" And no one answered him.
Jesus looked at them with anger, and he was very angry because their hearts were stubborn. Then he said to the man, "Stretch out your hand." He stretched it out, and his hand was good as new.
Then the Pharisees went out and began to plot with the Herodians against Jesus. For they had been sent to observe Jesus and report to the Pharisees and the Herodians. And the Pharisees and the Herodians agreed to find a way to destroy Jesus. And great crowds followed Jesus.
Jesus set out with his disciples for the Sea of Galilee, and a great crowd from Galilee followed him.
The people heard all that he was doing. Many came to him. From Judea, and from Jerusalem, and from Idumea. And from the territories that were beyond the Jordan. And from the region that was about Tyre and Sidon.
Because the crowd was large, Jesus told His disciples to prepare a small boat for Him. So that the people would not crowd Him.
Jesus healed many people. Therefore, the sick were pushing forward to touch him.
When the people saw him possessed by evil spirits, they fell to the ground. The spirits cried out, "You are the Son of God!"
But Jesus commanded them not to tell who he was. Jesus appoints the twelve apostles.
Jesus went up into a mountain: and he called to him certain whom he would, and they came to him.
And he appointed twelve of them and named them apostles. And from that time they were with him. And he also sent them to preach to the people.
They have authority over demons.
And Jesus said to the twelve, "Go away from the lake and stay in the region of the Gerasenes." So they went away.
There was James the son of Zebedee and his brother John. Jesus gave them the name Boanerges. Boanerges means Sons of Thunder.
And there were also Andrew, Philip, Bartholomew, Matthew, Thomas, James the son of Alphaeus. And there was also Thaddaeus and Simon the Zealot.
Judas Iscariot was one of them. He was the one who would later betray Jesus into the hands of his enemies. Jesus and Beelzebub
Jesus entered a house. Once again a crowd gathered. The crowd was so large that Jesus and his disciples were not even able to eat.
His family heard about this. So they went to take control. They said, "He is crazy."
There were some Pharisees. They came down from Jerusalem. They said, "He is controlled by Beelzebul! He is driving out demons by the power of the chief of demons."
Jesus said to them, "How can Satan cast out Satan?"
If a kingdom fights itself, it will not stand.
If the family disperses, it will not stand.
